      <p class="answer"><strong>The short answer:</strong> "Chartered Accountant" is a protected designation for members of the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI). "Accountant" is used far more loosely. Much everyday accounting does not need a CA, but some work, such as a company's statutory audit, does.</p>

      <h2 id="titles">Two words that sound alike</h2>
      <p><strong>A Chartered Accountant</strong> is a member of ICAI. The Chartered Accountants Act, 1949 makes it an offence for someone who is not a member to use the designation or claim to be one.</p>
      <p><strong>An accountant,</strong> in everyday speech, is anyone who keeps or prepares accounts. They may hold a commerce degree, be a skilled Tally operator with years of experience, or be a qualified professional of another kind. The word alone tells you little, so ask what it stands for.</p>
      <p>One trap: in specific laws "accountant" can have a narrow meaning. Under section 515 of the Income-tax Act, 2025, for instance, it means a Chartered Accountant holding a certificate of practice. Do not assume the everyday and legal meanings match.</p>

      <h2 id="practice">Not every CA is "in practice"</h2>
      <p>ICAI's Code of Ethics describes two classes of members: those in practice and those otherwise occupied. A member needs a <strong>certificate of practice</strong> from the Council to practise.</p>
      <p>So someone can truly be a CA while working in a company's finance team, without being entitled to practise. Claiming to be in practice without that certificate is also an offence under section 24 of the Act. If you need a CA for reserved work, the question to ask is whether they hold a current certificate of practice.</p>

      <h2 id="work">Who does which work</h2>
      <p>This table shows the usual position. It is a starting point, and the rules for your case may differ.</p>
      <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Who does which work, scrolls sideways on small screens">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Work</th><th scope="col">Does it need a CA?</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Day-to-day bookkeeping and reconciliations</th><td>Usually not</td><td>Skill and care matter most. A good bookkeeper may suit you better than a busy firm.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Preparing monthly reports for you</th><td>Usually not</td><td>Internal figures for your own use are not an audit or certificate.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Preparing GST or income tax returns</th><td>Not always</td><td>Accountants, tax and GST practitioners and CAs all do this. You stay responsible for what is filed.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Representing you on a GST matter</th><td>Not only a CA</td><td>CGST section 116 also lists advocates, cost accountants, company secretaries, authorised GST practitioners and others.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Representing you on an income tax matter</th><td>Not only a CA</td><td>Section 515 of the 2025 Act also lists legal practitioners and others, authorised by you in writing.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Statutory audit of a company</th><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Under section 141 of the Companies Act, 2013, an auditor must be a Chartered Accountant.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Tax audit, where it applies to you</th><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Section 63 of the Income-tax Act, 2025 requires the audit to be done by an "accountant", defined in section 515 as a CA holding a certificate of practice. Whether it applies to you depends on your turnover or receipts.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Certificates that a law requires from a CA</th><td><strong>Yes, where so stated</strong></td><td>Check the rule or form concerned. Other certificates may need a company secretary or cost accountant instead.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <h2 id="check">How to check a CA's credentials</h2>
      <p>This matters because the title is valuable, and impersonation does happen. These steps take a few minutes.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Ask for specifics:</strong> full name, ICAI membership number, the firm's registration number if a firm is involved, and whether they hold a certificate of practice.</li>
        <li><strong>Search {% ext "https://caconnect.icai.org/search-your-ca", "CA Connect" %},</strong> ICAI's portal for practising members and firms. ICAI says profiles there are synchronised with its database.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not treat a missing listing as proof of anything.</strong> ICAI asks practising members to register, so someone genuine may simply not have listed themselves. If in doubt, ask ICAI. The CA Connect page gives contact details for its Ethical Standards Board.</li>
        <li><strong>Check who will do the work.</strong> A firm may have a CA partner while your file is handled by others. That can be fine, but ask who reviews it and who signs.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="myths">Three things people get wrong</h2>
      <h3>"I need a CA for everything"</h3>
      <p>No. Paying for reserved-level skill for routine bookkeeping may not be needed, and many small businesses use a bookkeeper or accountant for regular work, with a CA for what requires one.</p>
      <h3>"My accountant can sign my audit"</h3>
      <p>Not unless they are eligible. Someone can prepare schedules and reconciliations for an audit, but that is preparation. Signing is a separate, restricted act.</p>
      <h3>"A CA means they know my trade"</h3>
      <p>The qualification does not tell you about experience with, say, marketplace settlements or clinic billing. Ask about clients like you.</p>

      <h2 id="choose">So which do you need?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>If a law or a lender names a CA, or the work must be certified or audited, start with a CA in practice.</li>
        <li>If you need regular records and returns, compare experienced accountants and CA firms on scope, fee and who does the work.</li>
        <li>If you have a notice or dispute, check who is authorised to represent you first.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you would like help working out what your work calls for, you can <a href="/request-an-accountant/">describe it to AccountOrbit</a>. We are a referral platform and not a CA firm, and we make no claim to supply CAs.</p>

